Ladybird’s ExtensionRegistry keeps the loaded extensions in the UI
process, persists them in Settings as id, directory, and enabled
state, and gives each install a UUID that never changes. Blink’s
ExtensionRegistry (extensions/browser/extension_registry.h) is the
same browser-process set, split into enabled, disabled, terminated,
and blocklisted ExtensionSets, with an observer list for load and
unload; its ids are hashes of the extension’s public key or install
path, so a page that can reach a chrome-extension:// URL learns a
public identity. Gecko’s ExtensionPolicyService registers one
WebExtensionPolicy per loaded extension in every process, and
Extension.sys.mjs assigns each install a per-profile UUID that
becomes the moz-extension:// host, so sites can’t fingerprint the
installed set; Ladybird takes that design. WebKit leaves the registry
to the embedder: WebExtensionController holds the loaded
WebExtensionContexts, and each context’s uniqueIdentifier is whatever
the embedder passes, which Safari persists per profile.
